# The pinned nidara-desktop release this repo packages.
#
# There used to be three more pins here — ASTAL_REF, AGS_REF and APPMENU_REF —
# kept in lockstep with nidara-desktop/install.sh, which carried its own copy of
# each. Nidara v0.8.0 removed the last of those upstreams (the application host,
# the bundler, every service and the PAM layer are native now), so install.sh has
# no `*_REF` at all and this file is the only place a pin lives.
#
# build-repo.sh builds `nidara` from this release TAG, with the PKGBUILD that
# ships INSIDE the tag (packaging/nidara/) — nothing about nidara's layout is
# committed here.
#
# Release runbook: tag nidara-desktop first, THEN point this at the new tag (one
# line) and push — CI fetches the tag's tarball, so a pin ahead of the tag fails.
# Empty = skip (releases predating the packaging switch).
NIDARA_REF=v0.10.0

# The pinned nidara-iso release whose `nidara-release` package this repo builds:
# the PRODUCT's identity (/etc/os-release) and therefore the PRODUCT's version,
# which is a different number from the one above and derives from nothing.
# nidara-iso/PRODUCT.md is the decision record.
#
# Release runbook, same shape as nidara's: tag nidara-iso first, THEN point this
# at the tag. Empty = skip.
#
# ⚠️ The fetch is an anonymous `curl` of the tag's tarball, so nidara-iso has to
# be PUBLIC for this to resolve. It was not until 2026-08-25, and a pin set while
# it was private would not have failed politely: the 404 takes down this whole
# build, the desktop's package included.
NIDARA_ISO_REF=v0.1.0
